+# Bootfile Studio service instructions
+
+The owner wants routine operations to run without daily supervision. Prepare and test ordinary reversible maintenance autonomously. Once release access is authorized, deploy under the documented release process and verify the deployed result and rollback path. Bring only material product, price, security, spending, or policy exceptions to the owner as concrete decisions.
+
+The current offer is $49 once for a solo service-business owner's Marketing Setup. Read `src/product.ts` and `README.md` before editing. Do not reintroduce the legacy personality-quiz proposition as the new offer, subscription pricing, invented business results, or a guarantee of income.
+
+All runtime capabilities default off until an explicitly configured app endpoint and scoped credential are verified. Keep secrets out of repository files, tool output, log records, and URLs. Do not retrieve credentials through a different provider to evade a hosting scope block.
+
+The service can persist pending drafts, invoke bounded paid operations, and trigger the app’s independent owned-blog review/publisher. Authorship never grants publication approval. The application owns the fixed facts/source policy, exact hashes, mandatory independent checks, two-review/two-publication rolling weekly caps, and separate enable flags. Routine compliant owned-blog publication proceeds under the verified operating charter without daily owner approval. Social publishing, GitHub push, ad spend, and outbound prospecting are absent.
+
+Never add startup generation, unbounded recursion, model-selected tool execution, dynamic credential destinations from content, or fail-open validation. Draft persistence must be confirmed; local files or a provider response do not count as a published article or a customer result.
+
+Run `npm test` before a release. Preserve meaningful boundary tests. Record the exact source SHA, Railway deployment, enabled capabilities, two unattended app run records, and any unresolved access limitation. Do not claim a prepared or committed change is active in production without that evidence.
